WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:03.000
Hi, everyone. Dan for BBC Learning English here with a

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.980
quick explanation of the difference between

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.100
'remember' and 'remind'.

00:00:07.109 --> 00:00:10.300
Now both 'remember' and 'remind' are verbs of memory,

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.460
but they are not the same.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.700
'Remember' is about having a memory and has

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.380
two verb patterns.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:19.320
Number one: remember + (verb)ing is used

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.960
when you remember something from the past.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:22.880
For example:

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.520
I remember saying 'Hi, everyone'.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:26.279
See?

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:29.000
Number two: remember + full infinitive is used when

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.980
something is to be done in the future.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:32.780
For example:

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.120
I must remember to do my shopping later.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.660
However, 'remind' means 'make someone remember'.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:40.620
Its verb pattern is

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.500
remind + somebody + a full infinitive verb.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.320
For example:

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.100
You must remind me to do my shopping later.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.040
Something can also remind someone of something.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.000
For example:

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.360
This cup reminds me of my other cup.

